explanation:
The body applies the library's generic `kostantToralPointsSubgroup` to the data that also define `groupScheme r`, `definingIdeal r` and `carrierι r`:
- the generators e_i = E_{i,i+1}, f_i = E_{i+1,i} and h_i = E_{ii} − E_{i+1,i+1};
- the standard representation `rep r`;
- the coordinate lattice ℤ^{r+1} with its standard basis;
- the weights ε_k.

The generic construction is the subgroup `hopfIdealPointsSubgroup` cut out by `kostantToralDefiningIdeal`. Concretely, a matrix belongs to it exactly when the corresponding A-point of GL_{r+1} vanishes on the defining Hopf ideal of the carrier. `points_def` records this. So the definition is the group of A-points of the carrier, as the docstring says. A is any commutative ring, and v is a free universe, so no generality is lost.

It is a specific object, namely the points of the specific type-A_r scheme. It is not a special case of some more general construction that the library failed to use: it is itself an instance of the library's general Kostant construction.

The module does not claim that these points equal SL_{r+1}(A) for every A. That identification is proved separately, and only over algebraically closed fields. So I do not cite Mathlib's `Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup` as an established counterpart, and nothing in the definition contradicts its docstring.
